# Model-View-Controller (MVC)
MVC is a pattern for organizing code within applications, particularly for user interfaces. It separates concerns into three components:
Model: handles data and business logic
View: manages presentation and user interface
Controller: orchestrates user input and coordinates between the Model and the View

This separation enables parallel development, where designers can focus on Views while developers work on Models. 
It also improves testability and provides clear code organization. 
Web frameworks like Ruby on Rails, Django, and ASP.NET MVC embrace this pattern, giving developers a shared vocabulary and predictable structure.

MVC differs from layered architecture. It can exist entirely within a presentation layer and does not enforce strict communication rules between components. 
Controllers tend to become bloated in complex applications, and modern single-page applications have evolved variations such as MVP and MVVM. 
Understanding MVC provides a foundation for more complex architectural patterns.
